    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Collagen vs whey protein is the wrong comparison, because collagen is an incomplete
        protein and will not build muscle the way whey does.</strong> Collagen contains no
        tryptophan at all and only about 2.5% leucine, against roughly 10% in whey, which is why
        whey wins for muscle growth without argument.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Whey for muscle.</strong> Around 25g of whey delivers close to 3g of leucine, the trigger dose for muscle protein synthesis. Collagen cannot reach it at any sane serving size.</li>
        <li><strong>Collagen for connective tissue.</strong> Roughly one amino acid in three is glycine, and proline is close behind. Tendon, ligament, cartilage and bone matrix are built from exactly that mix.</li>
        <li><strong>Different doses, different timing.</strong> Whey after training, 20g to 40g. Collagen at 10g daily, taken consistently, with the tendon work suggesting an hour before loading.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation.</strong> The connective tissue evidence rests on surrogate markers and modest pain trials, not on scans showing thicker tendons. Nobody has proven collagen makes a tendon stronger.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

