      <h2 id="is-collagen-complete">Is collagen a complete protein like whey?</h2>

      <p>
        No. Collagen is an incomplete protein because it lacks tryptophan entirely and is low in
        several other essential amino acids, so it cannot support muscle building on its own. Whey
        is a complete protein and contains every essential amino acid in generous amounts.
      </p>

      <p>
        A definition first, since the labels are used loosely. <strong>Protein is a chain of amino
        acids</strong>, twenty of which matter in human nutrition, nine of which your body cannot
        make and must obtain from food. Those nine are the essential amino acids. A protein source
        that supplies all nine in useful proportions is called complete. <strong>Collagen is the
        structural protein that holds the body together</strong>, making up skin, bone matrix,
        tendon, ligament and cartilage, and it has an unusual amino acid profile precisely because
        it is a structural rope rather than a metabolic building block. <strong>Whey is the protein
        fraction left over when milk is curdled</strong>, the liquid that separates from paneer, and
        it happens to be one of the most muscle-friendly proteins ever analysed.
      </p>

      <p>
        So when a collagen label says "10g protein", that figure is honest as chemistry and
        misleading as nutrition. The nitrogen is there. The tryptophan is not. Paul and colleagues
        published a full amino acid analysis in <em>Nutrients</em> in 2019 confirming the absence,
        and concluded that collagen peptides can sit comfortably inside a diet as long as the rest
        of the diet supplies the indispensable amino acids collagen does not.
      </p>

      <p>
        In my OPD this comes up in a particular way. A patient in his thirties tells me he has
        switched from whey to collagen because a reel told him collagen is the better protein. He
        has not switched to a better protein. He has switched to a different tool and stopped doing
        the job he was doing before.
      </p>

