      <h2 id="amino-profile">Collagen vs whey protein: the amino acid maths</h2>

      <p>
        The whole comparison collapses into two numbers: leucine and glycine. Whey is
        leucine-rich and glycine-poor. Collagen is the exact opposite, and that single inversion
        explains why one grows muscle and the other feeds connective tissue.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Leucine is the amino acid that switches muscle protein synthesis on.</strong> It
        behaves less like a brick and more like a signal, activating the mTOR pathway inside the
        muscle cell. Research on the leucine threshold puts the switching dose at roughly 2.5g to
        3g in a single feeding for a young adult, and higher in older adults, whose muscle responds
        more reluctantly. Whey is about 10% to 11% leucine by weight, so a 25g scoop lands right on
        that threshold without effort. Collagen is roughly 2.5% leucine. A 10g serving gives you
        somewhere near 250mg. To reach the threshold on collagen alone you would need to drink
        around 100g of it, which no product sells and no stomach would tolerate.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now the other direction. <strong>Glycine is the smallest amino acid</strong>, and it is the
        only one small enough to sit at every third position in the collagen triple helix without
        breaking the geometry. That is not a coincidence of biology, it is the reason the helix
        exists in that shape. Collagen is roughly one third glycine, with proline and
        hydroxyproline making up much of the rest. Whey contains very little of any of them.
        If you are trying to supply the raw material for tendon, ligament and cartilage matrix,
        whey is the wrong bag.
      </p>

