      </p>

      <h2 id="build-muscle">Will collagen build muscle if I take enough of it?</h2>

      <p>
        Not the way whey does, and the one head-to-head trial says so plainly. Oikawa and colleagues
        compared collagen peptides against whey protein in older women and found whey superior for
        preserving and building lean mass. Collagen is not a hypertrophy protein and no dose fixes
        the amino acid profile.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is a study people cite in the opposite direction, and it deserves an honest hearing
        rather than a quiet burial. Zdzieblik and colleagues, in the <em>British Journal of
        Nutrition</em> in 2015, gave 15g of collagen peptides daily to older men with sarcopenia
        who were also doing resistance training, and the collagen group gained more fat-free mass
        than placebo. That result is real. It is also easy to over-read. The comparison was collagen
        against nothing, not collagen against whey, and the participants were sarcopenic older men
        whose baseline protein intake was low. Add any protein to an undernourished 72-year-old who
        starts lifting and you should expect a change. It does not tell you that collagen beats whey
        in a 28-year-old already eating 1.6g of protein per kilo.
      </p>

      <p>
        My position, stated as an opinion rather than a finding: if muscle is your goal, the
        hierarchy is total daily protein first, resistance training second, whey timing a distant
        third, and collagen is not on that list at all. Collagen earns its place on a different
        list.
      </p>

