      <h2 id="what-collagen-does">What collagen does that whey does not</h2>

      <p>
        Collagen supplies glycine and proline to the tissues that are made of collagen, and it
        appears to signal those tissues to make more matrix. Whey does neither. That is the
        entire case for taking collagen, and it is a narrower case than most brands admit.
      </p>

      <p>
        Three lines of evidence support it, and I will grade them honestly.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Joint comfort trials are the strongest.</strong> Clark and colleagues followed 147
        athletes at Penn State for 24 weeks on 10g of collagen hydrolysate daily and found
        significant reductions in joint pain when walking, standing, carrying and lifting.
        Zdzieblik repeated the pattern in 2017 in young adults with functional knee pain, using 5g
        for 12 weeks. These are functional endpoints in real people, which is more than most
        supplement categories can produce. The fuller review sits in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Tendon synthesis markers are promising but indirect.</strong> Shaw and colleagues
        showed in the <em>American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em> in 2017 that 15g of gelatin
        with vitamin C, taken an hour before a short bout of loading, roughly doubled a blood marker
        of collagen synthesis. A blood marker is not a stronger tendon. It is a plausible mechanism
        with a measurable footprint, which is where honest reporting has to stop.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Bone matrix data is genuinely interesting.</strong> Bone is about one third organic
        matrix, and around 90% of that matrix is type I collagen. König and colleagues found in
        <em>Nutrients</em> in 2018 that 5g of collagen peptides daily for 12 months increased bone
        mineral density in postmenopausal women with reduced BMD. Whey has no comparable finding.
      </p>

