      </p>

      <h2 id="pre-training">The pre-training window, briefly</h2>

      <p>
        Take collagen with vitamin C about an hour before you load the tissue you care about. That
        is the whole protocol, and the reasoning is blood flow: tendon and ligament are poorly
        vascularised, so the amino acids need to be circulating when the loading briefly opens the
        supply.
      </p>

      <p>
        Shaw's design used 15g of gelatin plus 50mg of vitamin C, an hour of waiting, then six
        minutes of rope skipping. Vitamin C is not decoration here. It is required by the enzymes
        that hydroxylate proline and lysine, and without it your body cannot assemble stable
        collagen no matter how much you swallow. Skelecoll includes <strong>40mg of vitamin C from
        amla</strong> in the shot for that reason.
      </p>

      <p>
        I am keeping this deliberately short because the tendon and ligament protocol has its own
        article, written for the training population that asks about it most:
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-men">collagen for men, joints, recovery and skin</a>. If you
        are rehabbing a tendon, read that one rather than this section.
      </p>

      <h2 id="which-when">Collagen vs whey protein: which should you take?</h2>

      <p>
        Pick by the question you are trying to answer. If the question is "how do I get bigger or
        stronger", the answer is whey and total protein intake. If the question is "why do my knees
        hurt on stairs at 41", the answer is collagen, load management and a look at your vitamin D.
      </p>

      <p>Practically, by goal:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Muscle growth, strength, body recomposition.</strong> Whey. Aim for 1.6g to 2.2g of protein per kilo of bodyweight per day from all sources, with whey filling whatever gap food leaves.</li>
        <li><strong>Joint pain with activity, morning stiffness, creaky knees.</strong> Collagen at 10g daily for at least 12 weeks. Whey does nothing here.</li>
        <li><strong>Tendon or ligament rehabilitation.</strong> Collagen with vitamin C an hour before the loaded rehab session, on top of adequate total protein.</li>
        <li><strong>Bone density concerns, postmenopausal or over 60.</strong> Collagen plus calcium, vitamin D3 and magnesium. Read <a href="/pages/supplements-for-knee-pain">the full ingredient grading</a> before buying a stack.</li>
        <li><strong>Skin, hair, nails.</strong> Collagen, and only collagen. Whey has never been studied for this.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight management, appetite control.</strong> Whey. It is far more satiating per gram.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        On price, since this is India and the maths matters. A month of decent imported whey isolate
        runs somewhere between 3,000 and 5,000 rupees depending on brand and serving size. A daily
        collagen shot sits in a comparable band. They are not competing for the same budget line
        because they are not doing the same job, and if the budget only stretches to one, buy the
        one that matches your actual complaint. For most men under 35 that is whey. For most people
        over 45 with joint symptoms it is collagen.
      </p>

