      <h2 id="india">Protein in an Indian vegetarian diet</h2>

      <p>
        Whey is vegetarian and collagen is not, and in India that single fact settles the question
        for a large share of readers. Marine collagen comes from fish skin and scales. There is no
        vegetarian collagen, whatever a label implies.
      </p>

      <p>
        Products sold as "vegan collagen" contain no collagen. They are usually amino acid and
        vitamin C blends meant to support your own synthesis, which is a reasonable idea and a
        different product. If you are strictly vegetarian and want the connective tissue benefit,
        the honest options are those precursor blends, adequate total protein, and vitamin C, with
        the caveat that none of them has trial evidence resembling the collagen peptide studies.
        <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">How to boost collagen naturally</a> covers what
        actually holds up there.
      </p>

      <p>
        The bigger Indian problem is not which supplement to pick, it is the baseline. A vegetarian
        thali built around rice, roti and a modest bowl of dal can land well under a gram of protein
        per kilo. Dal is roughly 7g to 9g of protein per cooked cup, paneer about 18g per 100g,
        curd around 3g per 100g, and most people I ask are eating far less paneer than they think.
        Before either supplement is worth discussing, the food has to be counted. A collagen shot
        on top of a 45g-per-day protein intake in an 80kg man is decoration.
      </p>

      <p>
        One more India-specific point. Check for an FSSAI licence number on any protein you buy
        here, whichever kind it is. The adulteration reports in the domestic whey market have been
        bad enough that provenance is worth more than a discount.
      </p>

      <h2 id="both">Should you take both?</h2>

      <p>
        Yes, if you have both problems, and there is no interaction to worry about. They are
        digested identically, they compete for nothing, and taking them together is unremarkable.
        Take whey around training and collagen at whatever time you will actually remember.
      </p>

      <p>
        The people who genuinely need both are easy to identify. A 40-something who lifts three
        times a week and has a shoulder that objects to overhead pressing. A runner adding mileage
        whose knees are complaining before the muscle is. Anyone rehabbing a tendon while trying not
        to lose muscle. In each case whey answers the muscle question and collagen answers the
        connective tissue question, and neither substitutes for the other.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two caveats before you buy both. If you have a fish or shellfish allergy, marine collagen is
        out entirely. And if your total daily protein is already at target, adding 10g of collagen
        does not add 10g of usable protein to that target, because of the amino acid profile
        discussed above. Count it as a connective tissue supplement, not as protein. That is also
        why I would not recommend replacing a whey serving with a collagen serving in someone
        training hard, which is exactly what the reels suggest.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you are still deciding which collagen source suits you before any of this,
        <a href="/pages/types-of-collagen">types of collagen, 1 vs 2 vs 3</a> explains why a joint
        product and a skin product can contain the same molecule.
      </p>

