      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen better than whey protein?</summary>
          <p>
            Neither is better, they do different jobs. Whey builds muscle because it is a complete
            protein with about 10% leucine, while collagen supplies the glycine and proline that
            tendon, ligament, cartilage and bone matrix are made from. If muscle is the goal, whey
            wins outright.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen count towards my daily protein intake?</summary>
          <p>
            Only partially, and I would not count it. Collagen contains no tryptophan and is low in
            several essential amino acids, so 10g of collagen does not do the work of 10g of whey or
            dal. Treat it as a connective tissue supplement that happens to contain protein.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen and whey protein together?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, there is no interaction. Take whey around training for muscle repair and collagen
            at any consistent time of day, ideally about an hour before loading if you are working
            on a tendon. Many people over 40 who lift have reason to take both.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will collagen help me build muscle?</summary>
          <p>
            Not meaningfully. A head-to-head trial by Oikawa and colleagues found whey better than
            collagen peptides for lean mass in older women, and collagen supplies only about 250mg
            of leucine per 10g serving against the 2.5g to 3g needed to trigger muscle protein
            synthesis.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen protein vegetarian?</summary>
          <p>
            No. Marine collagen comes from fish and bovine collagen from cattle, so neither is
            vegetarian. Whey is vegetarian since it is a dairy fraction, and products marketed as
            vegan collagen contain amino acid precursors rather than collagen itself.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen should I take alongside my protein powder?</summary>
          <p>
            The trials that reduced joint pain used 5g to 10g of collagen peptides daily for 12
            weeks or more. Skelecoll provides 10,000mg per 30ml shot, which matches the dose used in
            the 24-week athlete trial by Clark and colleagues, and it sits on top of your normal
            protein intake rather than replacing part of it.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

